In the graph below each vertex is a country and each edge joins two countries that share a border.
The drawing is connected and has no crossings.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (P) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (Q) at (2.4,3.6);
\coordinate (R) at (4.4,2.0);
\coordinate (S) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0.8,0.2);
\draw (P)--(Q);
\draw (Q)--(R);
\draw (R)--(S);
\draw (S)--(T);
\draw (T)--(P);
\draw (P)--(S);
\draw (Q)--(S);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (T) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left] at (P) {$P$};
\node[above] at (Q) {$Q$};
\node[right] at (R) {$R$};
\node[below] at (S) {$S$};
\node[below left] at (T) {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use Euler's formula to find the number of faces, then check your answer by counting the regions.

